Profile-guided and link-time optimised builds of the n72 binary
================================================================

build.sh VARIANT builds touchHLE for the iPod touch 2G one of several ways,
each in its own target directory, target/n72-var/VARIANT:

  base    -O3, no LTO, no profile (how n72/env.sh's command builds it)
  gen     instrumented for a profile: the Rust code and OpenAL Soft, -O2
  o3pgo   -O3 with touchHLE.profdata
  o2lto   -O2, LTO, profile
  o3lto   -O3, LTO, profile

LTO means the Rust crates optimised as one unit (lto = "fat", one codegen
unit) and OpenAL Soft's objects optimised together at link time
(-flto, lld at the same level). OpenAL Soft follows the cargo profile's
optimisation level (its build.rs sets CMake's Release flags from OPT_LEVEL;
before, CMake's Release type made it -O3 whatever the profile said).

The profile runtime
-------------------

rustc 1.99 and Homebrew's clang are LLVM 23 and write raw profile format 11;
Alpine 3.24's compiler-rt (LLVM 22) has format 10, and rustup ships no
profiler_builtins for arm-unknown-linux-musleabihf. So the runtime is built
from compiler-rt's lib/profile of LLVM 23.1.1:
alpine-work/src/compiler-rt-23/build.sh -> libclang_rt.profile-armhf.a.
build.sh gen links it into the Rust side (-Z no-profiler-runtime under
RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP, which is why the gen build needs it) and gives clang a
copy of its resource directory with the runtime in it, so that CMake's
compiler checks for OpenAL Soft link.

The instrumented binary writes its counters every 256 frames
(cfg n72_pgo_gen in EAGLContext's presentRenderbuffer), since test runs end
with a kill:

  LLVM_PROFILE_FILE=/root/n72hle-test/pgo/th-%p.profraw ./touchHLE.gen ...
  llvm-profdata merge -o n72/pgo/touchHLE.profdata th-*.profraw

link-cc is the linker for these builds: the crate's cdylib (only Android
uses it) cannot be linked from code built for a static non-PIE executable
once profile counters keep every function in it, and is written anyway;
nothing loads it.

Checking output
---------------

altest.c renders six sources (sweep, noise, square, stereo chord; moving,
pitched, stopped and restarted) through an OpenAL loopback device with
n72/alsoft.conf and prints a checksum per second; altest.sh VARIANT links it
against that variant's OpenAL Soft. Run under qemu-arm -cpu arm1176 or on
the device: every variant must print the same checksums.

Results (2026-11-17, iPod on kernel #61, musl r50)
--------------------------------------------------

Profile: one Spore run of the gen build (start, menus, about a minute of
play, null OpenAL output), 21 MB raw. Hottest: OpenAL Soft's Mix_ and
DeviceBase::renderSamples, the cubic resampler, then touchHLE's guest
memory accessors and call path.

Output: altest prints the same checksums for all builds, under qemu and on
the device (20 s, every sample).

Spore, ms/frame over windows of 200 frames (N72_SVC_STATS, N72_FIXED_SEED,
ALSOFT_DRIVERS=null; the game is GPU-bound at 20 FPS throughout):

  build                main thread          mixer thread  runs
  base (-O3)           26.33                3.93          1
  o3pgo                26.70                3.93          1
  o2lto                26.40, 26.45 (part)  3.88          2
  o3lto                26.18, 26.23         3.85          2

The differences are within run-to-run noise (about 0.5 ms): no build is
worth installing. The iPod reset about 75 s into the second o2lto run (it
came back in iOS; no panic, no crumb, battery 95 % on USB). One event does
not pin it on that build, and the second base and o3pgo runs never ran.
altest's wall time on the device was not measured (busybox date has no %N).
